        "thinking": "I'm working through the first question, deriving गाङ् in yaṅ-pratyaya form — considering how the ī-kāra substitution rule for गा applies before kit/ṅit hal-ādi ārdhadhātuka affixes to get जेगीयते.\n\nNow I'm tracing the causative and luṅ derivation: after reduplication gives जेगीय, adding णिच् with अतो लोपः yields जेगीयि, then applying चङ् for luṅ with णि-lopa under the aniṭ rule, working through whether the reduplication for चङ् applies again to the already-reduplicated base.\n\napplies, so sanvadbhāva doesn't apply either. For reduplication, जे shortens to जि by the ec-to-ic rule, giving जिजेगीय्, and adding the अत् augment yields अजिजेगीयत्.\n\nChecking against known examples like बोभूयते giving अबुबोभूयत् confirms this reduplication pattern. Adding सम् gives समजिजेगीयत्, and I'm now working out the middle dual parasmaipada form with the तम् ending.\n\nI also need to verify whether णिच् allows parasmaipada here—yes, since णिचश्च makes it ubhayapada. Now I'm checking the ghumāsthā list to see if गा here refers to गाङ् or if गै (as in गायति) reduces to गा by the rule governing आदेच substitution, since that affects which roots fall under 6.4.66.\n\nMoving to दुष् (दिवादि, in the sense of vikṛti): the present form is दुष्यति, and I'm working out its यङ् form—applying guṇa in the reduplication to get दोदुष्य, then checking how णिच् interacts with the य of यङ् under the rule यस्य हलः, which deletes that य when preceded by a consonant in ārdhadhātuka contexts.\n\nI'm now considering whether दोषो णौ (6.4.90), which lengthens the upadhā of दुष् before णिच् to give दूषयति, should also apply to the reduplicated दोदुष् form, since that rule is normally stated for the simple dhātu.\n\nThinking of the parallel example बेभिदिता, where yaṅ-luk plus तृच् gives बेभिदिता without laghūpadha guṇa due to 1.1.4 — so by analogy दोदुष् + णिच् should similarly block guṇa and give दोदुषि. Now I need to check whether 6.4.90 \"दोषो णौ\" also applies here.\n\nI'm considering whether 6.4.90, which mandates ऊत् for the upadhā of दुष् before णि, actually extends to the yaṅanta form दोदुष् — since the rule is stated for the aṅga दुष् specifically, it's unclear whether tadantavidhi lets it apply to a form ending in दुष् like दोदुष्.\n\nI'm trying to figure out how an engine would check the dhātu's upadeśa form against the yaṅanta structure when deciding whether to apply the ūt substitution before ṇi, especially given the alternate option under 6.4.91 — it's genuinely unclear which rule would win here.\n\nI need to consider whether pratyaya-lakṣaṇa (1.1.62) keeps the yaṅ's effects alive after its lopa, since sthānivadbhāva proper only covers ac-ādeśa. After the यङ् is deleted, the resulting aṅga दोदुष् becomes what 6.4.90 operates on for दुष्.\n\nI'm trying analogous guṇa cases with yaṅanta formations — checking guh, rabh, han with their respective special sutras (7.1.63, 7.3.32, 7.3.54) to see how the frequentative and causative forms interact with these aṅga rules.\n\nNow I'm looking at how vidyut's codebase likely handles the दुष् root with णिच्, where it checks whether the following term is Ric, and wondering if the implementation skips over an empty yaṅ term when searching for the next relevant term.\n\nBoth rules sit in the asiddhavat section, so their relative ordering with respect to the same nimitta is unclear to me. I'm weighing whether 1.1.66's \"immediately preceding\" requirement for the saptamī in 6.4.90 is actually satisfied once the yaṅ vowel has been elided, since dūṣ would need to be directly adjacent to the causal affix for the rule to trigger.\n\nI'm distinguishing दुष् from the yaṅanta दोदुष्य by tadanta-vidhi under 1.1.72, since दोदुष्य only ends in दुष् after lopa, not before. I'm also trying to recall a parallel case with मृजेर्वृद्धिः (7.2.114) applied to yaṅanta मरीमृज्यते with णिच् to see if it supports the same pattern.\n\nLet me check how vidyut-prakriya actually implements the 6.4.90/91 rule in its source code...\n\nWriting more pseudocode...\n\nActually I should reason about this more on grammatical principles instead of implementation details. Since both yaṅ-lopa (6.4.49) and 6.4.90 are triggered by the same णिच् and operate within the asiddhavat domain (6.4.22), they're asiddha with respect to each other — meaning the lopa of य should be treated as not yet having occurred when 6.4.90 applies, so य still intervenes between दुष् and णि.\n\nGiven that, the aṅga for णि purposes is दोदुष्य, whose penultimate letter is य् rather than उ, so 6.4.90's condition isn't met and it doesn't apply. The result is दोदुषि.\n\nStill recalling implementation details... I'm trying to reason through whether vidyut would apply ūt lengthening here based on how it handles guṇa with yaṅ-luk and the ni-pratyaya check, but without certainty I'll lean toward दोदुषि as the grammatically sound form without ūt.\n\nConfirming the asiddhavat range covers both 6.4.49 and 6.4.90 under the same nimitta, so 6.4.49 stays asiddha for 6.4.90 — meaning no ūt applies and दोदुषि is correct. Now moving to सन् formation on दोदुषि, checking whether इट् is needed since सन् is ārdhadhātuka.\n\nWorking through the derivation, I'm checking whether reduplication applies afresh after ṇic and san the way it does in forms like अबुबोभूयत् — treating बोभूयि as the base and reduplicating its first ekāc \"बो\" to build बुबोभूय... before adding the desiderative suffix.\n\nThen for दुष् with ṇic+san, I'm tracing the parallel path: दो reduces to दु by the short-vowel rule, checking whether 7.4.80 forces इ instead, and confirming it doesn't apply here since the abhyāsa isn't followed by पु/यण्/ज्, landing on दुदोदुषयिष् before considering whether iṭ is needed before सन्.\n\nI'm second-guessing whether reduplication should take just the first vowel \"दो\" or extend to include the following consonant cluster, comparing with analogous cases like जागृ → जिजागरिषति and बुभूषति to figure out the right pattern for दोदुष्.\n\nLooking at